  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nSunday:\n\nModels hint at some remnant precip late tonight into Sunday \nmorning, but the forecast is mostly dry at this time. \nTemperatures and heat index values Sunday look similar to today.\n\nLate Sunday into early Monday heavy rainfall and flooding\nthreat: \n\nA strong upper ridge to the W will lead to relatively strong\nupper level flow from the NW while models now generally agree on\na surface low over Oklahoma and 850mb trough from the northern\nplains to over the surface low. This surface low leads to a\nstrong pressure gradient in the low levels, and a resulting SE\nLLJ. At the same time, an 850mb front will be stalled over the\nregion (currently looking like the southwestern CWA, but limited\nconfidence in location), with elevated instability above the\nfront. This should result in convection in vicinity of the front.\nThe strong S-SE low level flow counters the upper level flow to\nresult in slow storm motions that are near parallel to the \n850mb front, so also looking at potential for training storms. \nMoisture transport from the Gulf of America will be strong with\nthe low level flow, resulting in PWAT values over 2 inches, as \nshown by multiple models, which is 150-175% of normal. These \nfactors may lead to locally heavy rainfall and flooding, but\nmany factors need to come together for this. Uncertainty does \nexist, including in the unusual situation of a surface low \nsitting over Oklahoma in late August, the placement of the 850mb\nfront, the strength of the low level jet, and instability. \nCurrently, the NBM shows 50-60% PoPs over the southwestern CWA \nand 30-50% elsewhere. \n\nThrough the rest of next week, we will see some low end chances\nfor showers and storms as we generally remain under NW flow\naloft. Predictability will generally be low for any precip\nchances. \n\nThe pattern also keeps a temperature gradient across the area,\nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s NE to the mid to upper\n90s (along with heat index values in the upper 90s to mid \n100s) SW.",
